C. Wake Vortex Models of Rotorcraft
Wake vortices shed by rotorcraft are much more complex due to the rotor wake interaction. The literature on wake vortices for rotorcraft gives a less clear picture of wake vortex models for rotorcraft than for fixed-wing aircraft. The rotor acts as a aircraft wing in forward flight. Thus, rolled-up vortices are also shed by the rotor at the rotor tip. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft, the lift circulation strength of a rotorcraft varies circumferentially. This implies that the downwash varies as a function of the azimuth angle of the rotor. The airspeed that the rotor blades see varies circumferentially as a function of the azimuth angle Ψ. The tangential airspeed on the rotor blades is
VR = rΩ +V sin Ψ (12)
where rh ≤ r ≤ R where rh is the hub radius.
