This simulation demonstrates that the role of flight control is integral to recovery from wake vortex encounter. The stability and control characteristics of an aircraft can be substantially degraded when the aircraft is in wake. Flight control design has to ensure robustness to handle extreme cases of severe wake vortex encounters.
IV. Discussion
This study illustrates an important issue of spatial wake interactional dynamics for UAM. The UAM vision depicts a new airspace operation with many small aircraft flying in urban environments in close proximity to each other and building structures. For this vision to be realizable, spatial wake interaction should be addressed to ensure flight safety. The dynamics of many of these new vehicles are highly variable depending on their locations relative to other aircraft. The wake vortex dynamics are spatially distributed and time varying. Autonomy is envisioned as a future technology. Without understanding the physics of the aircraft operation, autonomy will not be able to mature. Many current studies of UAM and UAS tend to not address the issues of spatial distribution of the wake vortices or gust fields. In some cases, these studies do not examine the effects of spatial distribution of wake vortices on aerodynamic forces, stability, and control. Consequently, reduced stability and control effectiveness are not accounted for in these studies. The spatial distribution can impact aircraft stability and control as demonstrated in this study. The problem will become magnified when the airspace is more crowded with many aircraft flying close together. Recommendations for future research are needed to ensure UAM will be a viable vision.
V. Conclusion
This paper presents a study on spatial wake interactional dynamics of both fixed-wing aircraft and rotorcraft for urban air mobility. The Proctor wake vortex model is validated against flight data for Boeing 757 and thus is used for the study. A propose method for computing the unsteady lift circulation for rotorcraft is presented to account for unsteady aerodynamics and the compressibility effect due to the rotor speed. The corrections make use of the Theodorsen’s theory and a new proposed method for the compressibility correction to Theodorsen’s theory. This method can compute a realistic lift circulation for use in estimating the wake-induced downwash of a rotorcraft. A vehicle-building wake interaction is studied using the method of image which can provide a quick and efficient way for estimating the wake vortex interaction with buildings. The effects of wake vortices on aerodynamic loads, stability, and control are examined. Wake-induced lift and rolling moment are a safety concern. The other effect is the reduced stability and control of aircraft which is demonstrated in the study. A flight control simulation is performed to illustrate the role of flight control during a wake vortex encounter. The stability and control characteristics of an aircraft can
